<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fueros de Aragón</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1247</span> · Also known as Code of Huesca, Compilation of Huesca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fueros de Aragón are the compilation of the customary laws of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a> made at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> in 1247 under James I, prepared by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Vidal de Canellas of Huesca. Replacing a variety of local laws, they became the general law of the kingdom, extended by later legislation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes">Cortes</a>. They remained in force until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees">Nueva Planta decrees</a> of 1707, and Aragonese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> still derives from them.</p></div>
